.com/users/StuartHarris/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/StuartHarris/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/StuartHarris/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/StuartHarris","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/redbadger/crux/commits/1bf4bb10698c66b843124db77790297dcdb51e2d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-03T13:06:14Z","email":"stuart.harris@red-badger.com","name":"Stuart Harris"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-03T13:09:52Z","email":"stuart.harris@red-badger.com","name":"Stuart Harris"},"message":"feat(crux_http): expose HttpResponse -> Response conversion\n\nAn app-defined `Operation` that answers with `HttpResult` — an upload the\nshell performs, whose body the core never held — could not reach the\nconversion `Http` requests use internally: `Response::new` is `pub(crate)`\nand `HttpError::Http` is `#[non_exhaustive]`. Re-interpreting status codes in\nthe app is the wrong answer, because the whole point of reusing `HttpResult`\nis that the app handles such a response exactly as it handles one from\n`Effect::Http`.\n\n`TryFrom<HttpResponse> for Response<Vec<u8>>` composes the two steps that were\nalready there — `RawResponse::try_from` and `Response::new` — so a 4xx or 5xx\nstill arrives as `HttpError::Http` carrying the server''s own message, from the\nsingle place that invariant lives.","tree":{"sha":"a28630f75e2a1965e21d6fd9254043562858c34e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/redbadger/crux/git/trees/a28630f75e2a1965e21d6fd9254043562858c34e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/redbadger/crux/git/commits/1bf4bb10698c66b843124db77790297dcdb51e2d","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/203552?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/StuartHarris/events{/privacy}",